haiku/arm64: find g safely when a signal lands in libroot (issue 843)

haiku/arm64 keeps g only in R28 -- with CGO_ENABLED=0 runtime·load_g is a
no-op, so sigtramp hands sigtrampgo whatever R28 held when the signal
arrived. Go code never uses R28 for anything else, but libroot is C code and
x19-x28 are ordinary callee-saved registers to it: realloc/omalloc and
__allocate_pages use x28 as a local while they run. A SIGURG async
preemption that lands in there (in practice: during the malloc that
pthread_create and semacreate do, or a _kern_mutex_lock under it) gives
sigtrampgo g = 0x1 or 0xffff. Dereferencing g.m faults inside the handler,
where Go's sa_mask blocks SIGSEGV, so the faulting load is retried forever:
a thread pinned at ~30% user / ~70% kernel, no syscalls, SIGQUIT never
handled, and the rest of the process wedged behind it (STW, malloc lock).

Fix it the way Linux handles a signal inside the VDSO: minit stores the M's
g0 at the bottom of its gsignal stack, and sigFetchG returns that whenever
the signal interrupted libroot. libroot only ever runs on g0 (or on gsignal
with signals blocked), so g0 is the correct g there.

"Interrupted libroot" is decided by sigInLibroot: the PC is outside Go text,
and the signal is not an instruction-fetch fault that Go itself caused. A
call through a zeroed funcval jumps to PC 0 (or a corrupt one to any
unmapped address); no instruction there ran, R28 still holds the caller's g,
and sighandler needs that g to raise a recoverable panic. Such a fault is a
SIGSEGV/SIGBUS whose fault address equals the PC, with LR in Go text.

When the signal stack is not a heap span (GODEBUG=efence=1, stackFromSystem)
there is nowhere to read g0 from, so sigFetchG returns nil, as it does for
the Linux VDSO. It must not fall back to R28: in libroot that is the
clobbered value this patch exists to avoid. sigtrampgo already drops a
SIGPROF or SIGURG that arrives with no g, so only a synchronous fault taken
inside libroot reaches badsignal.

minit writes g0 at gsignal.stack.lo, and sigFetchG finds that same address
from the handler's SP as the start of the stack span element holding it, so
writer and reader agree whether the signal stack has a span to itself or was
carved from a stackpool span. The Linux VDSO read uses the same lookup; for
its dedicated 32 KiB gsignal span the result is unchanged.

Native Go toolchains, and every Go binary built with them, need rebuilding
for the fix to take effect (the golang hpkg, amazon-ssm-agent).

diff --git a/src/runtime/os2_haiku.go b/src/runtime/os2_haiku.go
index 05f4b3b..a617061 100644
--- a/src/runtime/os2_haiku.go
+++ b/src/runtime/os2_haiku.go
@@ -237,11 +237,26 @@ func miniterrno()
 // Called to initialize a new m (including the bootstrap m).
 // Called on the new thread, cannot allocate memory.
 func minit() {
+	// Record this M's g0 at the bottom of its signal stack before any
+	// signal is unblocked. A signal that lands while libroot code is
+	// running cannot trust R28 to hold g (see sigFetchG), and every
+	// libroot call runs on g0, so sigFetchG reads the g0 back from here.
+	// It finds this address from the handler's SP alone, as the start of
+	// the stack span element that holds SP, which is stack.lo for any
+	// heap-allocated stack. (An efence stack has no span; sigFetchG then
+	// returns nil, which sigtrampgo tolerates for SIGURG and SIGPROF.)
+	// The word is below every frame the signal handler can use, which is
+	// also where Linux keeps g across a VDSO call. It is stored as a
+	// uintptr because there is no P here to run a write barrier; m.g0
+	// keeps the g reachable.
+	mp := getg().m
+	*(*uintptr)(unsafe.Pointer(mp.gsignal.stack.lo)) = uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(mp.g0))
+
 	asmcgocall(unsafe.Pointer(abi.FuncPCABI0(miniterrno)), unsafe.Pointer(&libc__errnop))
 
 	minitSignals()
 
-	getg().m.procid = uint64(pthread_self())
+	mp.procid = uint64(pthread_self())
 }
 
 // Called from dropm to undo the effect of an minit.
diff --git a/src/runtime/signal_haiku_arm64.go b/src/runtime/signal_haiku_arm64.go
index 709ef87..36c0507 100644
--- a/src/runtime/signal_haiku_arm64.go
+++ b/src/runtime/signal_haiku_arm64.go
@@ -71,3 +71,33 @@ func (c *sigctxt) set_r28(x uint64) { c.regs().x[28] = x }
 
 func (c *sigctxt) set_sigcode(x uint64) { c.info.si_code = int32(x) }
 func (c *sigctxt) set_sigaddr(x uint64) { c.info.si_addr = uintptr(x) }
+
+// sigInLibroot reports whether the signal described by c interrupted C code
+// in libroot (or another loaded image) rather than Go, so that R28 cannot be
+// trusted to hold g and sigFetchG must use the g0 that minit stored instead.
+//
+// A PC outside Go text is not enough on its own: Go code that calls through a
+// zeroed funcval jumps to PC 0, and a corrupt function value can land on any
+// unmapped address. That faults on the instruction fetch, so no instruction
+// at the PC ever ran and R28 still holds the caller's g; sighandler has to
+// see that g to turn the fault into a recoverable panic rather than a fatal
+// "unexpected signal during runtime execution". Such a fault is a SIGSEGV
+// or SIGBUS whose fault address is the PC itself (Haiku reports FAR, which
+// equals ELR for an instruction abort, and ELR for a misaligned PC), and the
+// branch that got there left LR pointing into Go text. Both together mean
+// "Go jumped somewhere bad" and keep R28. A fetch fault reached from C code
+// leaves LR in C, and an asynchronous signal (SIGURG, SIGPROF) never has a
+// fault address equal to an executing PC, so neither is excluded.
+//
+//go:nosplit
+func sigInLibroot(c *sigctxt) bool {
+	pc := c.sigpc()
+	if findmoduledatap(pc) != nil {
+		return false
+	}
+	if sig := uint32(c.info.si_signo); (sig == _SIGSEGV || sig == _SIGBUS) &&
+		c.fault() == pc && findmoduledatap(c.siglr()) != nil {
+		return false
+	}
+	return true
+}
